  there, return it; otherwise generate a UUID, store it, return it. Every later form calls the same function and gets
  the same ID. That read-through shape is what makes "works across the app" true without any coordination between forms
  — there's no init step to forget and no ordering requirement beyond the script tag.

  ID generation prefers [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m, falling back to a [38;5;153mcrypto.getRandomValues[39m UUIDv4. The fallback isn't
  theoretical: [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID[39m requires a secure context, and you chose to keep the page openable over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, where
  that guarantee varies by browser.

  [1mModified: [38;5;153mindex.html[0m — one line, [38;5;153m<script src="tracking.js"></script>[39m immediately before the existing [38;5;153mapp.js[39m tag at
  line 13. Ordering matters because both are classic scripts; that's the cost we accepted in the last decision.

  [1mModified: [38;5;153mapp.js[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m becomes [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m, with [38;5;153muserId[39m appended last so existing two-argument
  calls keep working. The form handler at line 23 becomes [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, Tracking.getUserId())[39m.

  Note I'm keeping [38;5;153mlogin[39m a pure function of its arguments rather than having it call [38;5;153mTracking.getUserId()[39m internally —
  that's your "caller passes it" answer, and it's the right call: it keeps [38;5;153mlogin[39m testable without stubbing browser
  storage.

  [1mTooling question (part of this section)[0m

  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts, no dependencies, and no devDependencies — no linter, no formatter, no test runner. This
  is the cheapest moment to add any of that, before the code exists. A persisted-ID module with fallback paths is
  exactly the kind of thing that deserves a unit test, and right now there's no place to put one.

  Whatever you pick lands in the spec's Global Constraints so the implementation inherits it.
